window.META_MATH = {"subject": "Algebraic Topology", "projects": [{"id": "AT-01", "slug": "fundamental-group-of-the-circle", "title": "Fundamental Group of the Circle", "target": "<p>Prove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(S^1,1)\\cong\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing the covering map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p:\\mathbb R\\to S^1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=e^{2\\pi it}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use the covering\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p:R\\to S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=e^{(2\\pi it)}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nImport path lifting with prescribed initial value and homotopy lifting\nfor a covering map: a path, or a homotopy of paths, has a unique\ncontinuous lift after an initial lift is fixed. Here evenly covered arcs\nsupply the local inverses, and compact subdivision supplies global\nlifting. Based loops start/end at 1; their chosen lifts start at 0.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Winding number is well-defined", "proof": "1. Sincep(αtilde(1))=1, the endpoint lies inZ.\n\n2. For a based homotopy, its lift has initial endpoint0 throughout; the terminal lifted endpoint is a continuous integer-valued function of the homotopy parameter, hence constant.\n\n3. Thusw([α])=αtilde(1) depends only on the based homotopy class.", "role": "<p>Read the integer endpoint and audit based homotopies.</p>", "inputs": "<p>A based loop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nand its lift\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde{\\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfoundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>An integer winding invariant on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}(S^{1},1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E1/v1: An integer winding invariant on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}(S^{1},1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain A based loop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nand its lift\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde{\\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfoundational imports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions\nattached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Read the integer endpoint and audit based homotopies.</p>", "Validation": "<p>The basepoint must remain fixed during the homotopy.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\widetilde{\\alpha}(1))=1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe endpoint lies in Z.</p>", "<p>For a based homotopy, its lift has initial endpoint0 throughout; the\nterminal lifted endpoint is a continuous integer-valued function of the\nhomotopy parameter, hence constant.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w([\\alpha ])=\\widetilde{\\alpha}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>\ndepends only on the based homotopy class.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>The basepoint must remain fixed during the homotopy. Publish only\nafter checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: The basepoint must remain fixed during\nthe homotopy. Recheck dependent nodes N2, N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Group law and surjectivity", "proof": "1. If lifts of α andβ end at m andn, a lift of the concatenation is the lift of α followed by the lift ofβ translated bym; its endpoint is m+n.\n\n2. Hencew is a homomorphism.\n\n3. For eachn∈Z the loopα_n(t)=e^(2πint) has liftnt and windingn, proving surjectivity.", "role": "<p>Translate the second lift before concatenating.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Two lifted loops and their endpoints; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>A surjective homomorphism\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w:\\pi _{1}(S^{1},1)\\to Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E2/v1: A surjective homomorphism\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w:\\pi _{1}(S^{1},1)\\to Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Two lifted loops and their endpoints; prior exports E1/v1;\nkeep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Translate the second lift before concatenating.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Concatenating two lifts both based at 0 would create a discontinuity\nunless the first winding is 0.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>If lifts of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math>\nend at m andn, a lift of the concatenation is the lift of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nfollowed by the lift of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math>\ntranslated bym; its endpoint is m+n.</p>", "<p>Hencew is a homomorphism.</p>", "<p>For each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>\nthe loop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha _n(t)=e^{(2\\pi int)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas liftnt and windingn, proving surjectivity.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Concatenating two lifts both based at 0 would create a discontinuity\nunless the first winding is 0. Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Concatenating two lifts both based at\n0 would create a discontinuity unless the first winding is 0. Recheck\ndependent nodes N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Trivial kernel", "proof": "1. Ifw([α])=0 thenαtilde(0)=αtilde(1)=0.\n\n2. The homotopyH(s,t)=p((1−s)αtilde(t)) is continuous, remains based at 1 at both endpoints, equalsα when s=0, and is the constant loop when s=1.\n\n3. Thus α is null-homotopic.\n\n4. The kernel is trivial, so the surjective homomorphism is injective and π₁(S¹,1)≅Z.", "role": "<p>Contract the closed lifted loop and project.</p>", "inputs": "<p>A lift with endpoint0; prior exports E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>The fundamental group of the circle with its explicit winding\nisomorphism.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E3/v1: The fundamental group of the circle with its explicit\nwinding isomorphism.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain A lift with endpoint0; prior exports E2/v1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Contract the closed lifted loop and project.</p>", "Validation": "<p>A projected contraction must preserve both loop endpoints.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w([\\alpha ])=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde{\\alpha}(0)=\\widetilde{\\alpha}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The homotopy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(s,t)=p((1-s)\\widetilde{\\alpha}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>\nis continuous, remains based at 1 at both endpoints, equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand is the constant loop when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nis null-homotopic.</p>", "<p>The kernel is trivial, so the surjective homomorphism is injective\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}(S^{1},1)\\cong Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>A projected contraction must preserve both loop endpoints. Publish\nonly after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: A projected contraction must preserve\nboth loop endpoints. Withhold this terminal result until its gate\npasses.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Group law and surjectivity", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Two lifted loops and their endpoints; prior\nexports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Trivial kernel", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: A lift with endpoint0; prior exports\nE2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-winding-number-is-well-defined-1\">§4.N1 — Winding number is\nwell-defined</h3>\n<p>For a based loop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe its unique lift starting at zero.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\widetilde{\\alpha}(1))=1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe endpoint lies in Z.</p></li>\n<li><p>For a based homotopy, its lift has initial endpoint0 throughout;\nthe terminal lifted endpoint is a continuous integer-valued function of\nthe homotopy parameter, hence constant.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w([\\alpha ])=\\widetilde{\\alpha}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>\ndepends only on the based homotopy class.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-group-law-and-surjectivity-1\">§4.N2 — Group law and\nsurjectivity</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>If lifts of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math>\nend at m andn, a lift of the concatenation is the lift of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nfollowed by the lift of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math>\ntranslated bym; its endpoint is m+n.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hencew is a homomorphism.</p></li>\n<li><p>For each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>\nthe loop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha _n(t)=e^{(2\\pi int)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas liftnt and windingn, proving surjectivity.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-trivial-kernel-1\">§4.N3 — Trivial kernel</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w([\\alpha ])=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde{\\alpha}(0)=\\widetilde{\\alpha}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The homotopy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(s,t)=p((1-s)\\widetilde{\\alpha}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>\nis continuous, remains based at 1 at both endpoints, equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand is the constant loop when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nis null-homotopic.</p></li>\n<li><p>The kernel is trivial, so the surjective homomorphism is\ninjective and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}(S^{1},1)\\cong Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>The loop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{(6\\pi it)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas lift3t and winding3. Reversing it gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3</annotation></semantics></math>;\nconcatenating it with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{(-4\\pi it)}</annotation></semantics></math>\ngives winding1. The covering itself identifies endpoint changes rather\nthan relying on a picture of turns.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>An open path ending at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlifts to an endpoint in 1/2+Z, notZ, so it does not define this loop\ninvariant. Validation checks the endpoint and based-homotopy conditions\nbefore exporting an integer.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Contract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge + Strategy:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Contract:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>An open path ending at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlifts to an endpoint in 1/2+Z, notZ, so it does not define this loop\ninvariant. Validation checks the endpoint and based-homotopy conditions\nbefore exporting an integer.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "9d0066b95d108a3b5293fed41e2cd098d0cf0c055f9554d0aeb9a53ab0d8df9e", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of fundamental group of the circle,\nwith explicit mathematical contracts, local verification gates, and\nreusable exports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf\">Allen\nHatcher, Algebraic Topology: covering spaces, cellular homology, and cap\nproducts</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/fundamental-group-of-the-circle.pdf"}, {"id": "AT-02", "slug": "fundamental-group-of-torus", "title": "Fundamental Group of the Torus via Seifert–van Kampen", "target": "<p>For the torus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\langle a,b\\mid aba^{-1}b^{-1}=1\\rangle\\cong\\mathbb Z^2.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The output is a fundamental-group presentation followed by an\nexplicit group isomorphism. A drawing of two loops or the assertion that\nthey commute is not a proof that these are all generators and\nrelations.</p>", "imports": ["<p>a based loop is considered modulo based homotopy; multiplication is\nloop concatenation. Import\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(S^1)\\cong\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\nhomotopy invariance, and Seifert–van Kampen for path-connected open sets\nwith path-connected intersection. For such a cover, the theorem gives\nthe group pushout: relations identify the two images of the intersection\ngroup. We explicitly construct the cover used for the torus’s two-cell\nattachment.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "The Square Model and Its Boundary Word", "proof": "Represent the torus by a square with opposite edges identified in the same direction. All four vertices become one vertex, and the horizontal and vertical edge pairs become loops a and b. The one-skeleton Y is the wedge $S^1\\vee S^1$.\n\nThe remaining open square is one two-cell. Traversing its boundary counterclockwise gives horizontal a, vertical b, reverse horizontal a^(−1), and reverse vertical b^(−1). Hence its attaching loop is\n\n$$w=aba^{-1}b^{-1}.$$\n\nChanging consistent orientation conventions can invert or conjugate the word without changing its normal closure. Inconsistent conventions, however, can produce the wrong space.", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E1/v1: The torus has one vertex, loops\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand one two-cell attached by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">aba^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Quotient square and orientations</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Separate one-skeleton and face</p>", "Validation": "<p>Identify all four vertices</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>The Square Model and Its Boundary Word. Establish the following local\nresult: The torus has one vertex, loops\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand one two-cell attached by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">aba^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>The quotient square with opposite edges consistently identified.</p>", "export": "<p>The torus has one vertex, loops\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand one two-cell attached by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">aba^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Track all four vertices to the common vertex.</p>", "<p>Choose positive horizontal and vertical loop orientations.</p>", "<p>Read the counterclockwise boundary word with its inverse edges.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Identify all four vertices Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Identify all four vertices Recheck\ndependent nodes N2, N3, N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Compute the One-Skeleton Group", "proof": "Cover the wedge by one open set containing the entire first circle and short arcs around the wedge point on the second, and a second set with the roles reversed. Each deformation retracts onto its full circle. Their intersection is a small cross-shaped tree around the common vertex, which is contractible and path connected.\n\nVan Kampen therefore gives\n\n$$\\pi_1(Y)\\cong\\mathbb Z*\\mathbb Z=F(a,b),$$\n\nthe free group on a and b. Before the face is filled, the generators do not commute; the commutator is a nontrivial reduced word in this free group.", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E2/v1: The one-skeleton has fundamental group\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Circle group and van Kampen</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Use wedge-of-circles cover</p>", "Validation": "<p>Do not assume a and b commute</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Compute the One-Skeleton Group. Establish the following local result:\nThe one-skeleton has fundamental group\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1, the circle group, and Seifert–van Kampen.</p>", "export": "<p>The one-skeleton has fundamental group\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Use two open sets retracting to the individual circles.</p>", "<p>Check their intersection is a contractible tree.</p>", "<p>The pushout is the free product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>*</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Z*\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\nbefore imposing a face relation.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Do not assume a and b commute Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Do not assume a and b commute Recheck\ndependent nodes N3, N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "A Valid Open Cover for the Two-Cell Attachment", "proof": "Write X for Y with the closed disk attached along w; this is the square model of the torus. Choose an interior center o of the attached disk.\n\nLet U=X\\{o}. This is open and deformation retracts onto Y: radially push each point in the punctured disk toward its boundary while fixing Y. Let V be a smaller open disk around o lying wholly inside the two-cell. It is open and contractible. These sets cover X. Their intersection is a punctured smaller disk, path connected, with fundamental group Z.\n\nChoose a basepoint in that intersection and a path in U to the original vertex. A positive generator of the intersection group, transported to Y by the radial retraction, becomes w up to this basepoint conjugation. Its image in V is trivial because V is contractible.\n\nVan Kampen identifies the U-image with the trivial V-image. Consequently\n\n$$\\pi_1(X)\\cong F(a,b)/\\langle\\!\\langle w\\rangle\\!\\rangle,$$\n\nwhere the double brackets mean the normal subgroup generated by w. Normal closure is required because the fundamental group relation also kills all conjugates of the attaching loop. This proves the stated presentation and shows that the single two-cell introduces exactly that normal relation.", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E3/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)=F(a,b)/\\langle\\!\\langle aba^{-1}b^{-1}\\rangle\\!\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Open-cover pushout</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Fill the two-cell</p>", "Validation": "<p>Check open sets and connected intersection</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>A Valid Open Cover for the Two-Cell Attachment. Establish the\nfollowing local result:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)=F(a,b)/\\langle\\!\\langle aba^{-1}b^{-1}\\rangle\\!\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1, N2/E2, and van Kampen with a valid open cover.</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)=F(a,b)/\\langle\\!\\langle aba^{-1}b^{-1}\\rangle\\!\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Take the complement of the face center and a small open disk around\nthat center.</p>", "<p>The punctured overlap is path connected; its generator maps to the\nattaching word and to the identity respectively.</p>", "<p>Form the normal closure relation, including the basepoint\nconjugation.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Check open sets and connected intersection Publish only after\nchecking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Check open sets and connected\nintersection Recheck dependent nodes N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Identify the Presented Group with Integer Pairs", "proof": "Let $P=\\langle a,b\\mid aba^{-1}b^{-1}=1\\rangle$. The relation is equivalent to ab=ba. Define\n\n$$\\Phi:P\\to\\mathbb Z^2,\\qquad\\Phi(a)=(1,0),\\quad\\Phi(b)=(0,1).$$\n\nIt is well-defined because the commutator maps to zero in the abelian target. Define the opposite map\n\n$$\\Psi:\\mathbb Z^2\\to P,\\qquad\\Psi(m,n)=a^mb^n.$$\n\nSince a and b commute, Psi respects addition: $a^mb^na^rb^s=a^{m+r}b^{n+s}$. Phi composed with Psi is the identity on integer pairs. Psi composed with Phi is the identity on the generators a,b and hence on all of P. Thus the maps are inverse isomorphisms. No extra relation between the integers remains.\n\nCombining N1–N4 proves $\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2$. $\\square$", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E4/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Presentations and integer pairs</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Construct inverse group maps</p>", "Validation": "<p>Prove no additional hidden relations</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E4/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Identify the Presented Group with Integer Pairs. Establish the\nfollowing local result:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N3/E3 and the universal property of a group presentation.</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math>\nto the standard integer coordinate vectors.</p>", "<p>Define the reverse map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(m,n)\\mapsto a^mb^n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nusing commutativity.</p>", "<p>Check both composites are identities, ruling out further integer\nrelations.</p>"], "exportId": "E4", "gate": "<p>Prove no additional hidden relations Publish only after checking\nevery displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N4: Prove no additional hidden relations\nWithhold this terminal result until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Compute the One-Skeleton Group", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1, the circle group, and Seifert–van\nKampen.</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: A Valid Open Cover for the Two-Cell Attachment", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1, N2/E2, and van Kampen with a valid\nopen cover.</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N3: A Valid Open Cover for the Two-Cell Attachment", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1, N2/E2, and van Kampen with a valid\nopen cover.</p>"}, {"source": "N3", "target": "N4", "export": "E3/v1", "id": "T4", "activity": "N4: Identify the Presented Group with Integer Pairs", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N3/E3 and the universal property of a group\npresentation.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-the-square-model-and-its-boundary-word-1\">N1 — The Square\nModel and Its Boundary Word</h3>\n<p>Represent the torus by a square with opposite edges identified in the\nsame direction. All four vertices become one vertex, and the horizontal\nand vertical edge pairs become loops a and b. The one-skeleton Y is the\nwedge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∨</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^1\\vee S^1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p>The remaining open square is one two-cell. Traversing its boundary\ncounterclockwise gives horizontal a, vertical b, reverse horizontal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand reverse vertical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nHence its attaching loop is</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=aba^{-1}b^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Changing consistent orientation conventions can invert or conjugate\nthe word without changing its normal closure. Inconsistent conventions,\nhowever, can produce the wrong space.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n2-compute-the-one-skeleton-group-1\">N2 — Compute the\nOne-Skeleton Group</h3>\n<p>Cover the wedge by one open set containing the entire first circle\nand short arcs around the wedge point on the second, and a second set\nwith the roles reversed. Each deformation retracts onto its full circle.\nTheir intersection is a small cross-shaped tree around the common\nvertex, which is contractible and path connected.</p>\n<p>Van Kampen therefore gives</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>*</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(Y)\\cong\\mathbb Z*\\mathbb Z=F(a,b),</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>the free group on a and b. Before the face is filled, the generators\ndo not commute; the commutator is a nontrivial reduced word in this free\ngroup.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n3-a-valid-open-cover-for-the-two-cell-attachment-1\">N3 — A\nValid Open Cover for the Two-Cell Attachment</h3>\n<p>Write X for Y with the closed disk attached along w; this is the\nsquare model of the torus. Choose an interior center o of the attached\ndisk.</p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=X\\setminus\\{o\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis is open and deformation retracts onto Y: radially push each point\nin the punctured disk toward its boundary while fixing Y. Let V be a\nsmaller open disk around o lying wholly inside the two-cell. It is open\nand contractible. These sets cover X. Their intersection is a punctured\nsmaller disk, path connected, with fundamental group Z.</p>\n<p>Choose a basepoint in that intersection and a path in U to the\noriginal vertex. A positive generator of the intersection group,\ntransported to Y by the radial retraction, becomes w up to this\nbasepoint conjugation. Its image in V is trivial because V is\ncontractible.</p>\n<p>Van Kampen identifies the U-image with the trivial V-image.\nConsequently</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(X)\\cong F(a,b)/\\langle\\!\\langle w\\rangle\\!\\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where the double brackets mean the normal subgroup generated by w.\nNormal closure is required because the fundamental group relation also\nkills all conjugates of the attaching loop. This proves the stated\npresentation and shows that the single two-cell introduces exactly that\nnormal relation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n4-identify-the-presented-group-with-integer-pairs-1\">N4 —\nIdentify the Presented Group with Integer Pairs</h3>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\langle a,b\\mid aba^{-1}b^{-1}=1\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe relation is equivalent to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ab=ba</annotation></semantics></math>.\nDefine</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi:P\\to\\mathbb Z^2,\\qquad\\Phi(a)=(1,0),\\quad\\Phi(b)=(0,1).</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>It is well-defined because the commutator maps to zero in the abelian\ntarget. Define the opposite map</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>→</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Psi:\\mathbb Z^2\\to P,\\qquad\\Psi(m,n)=a^mb^n.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Since a and b commute, Psi respects addition:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^mb^na^rb^s=a^{m+r}b^{n+s}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nPhi composed with Psi is the identity on integer pairs. Psi composed\nwith Phi is the identity on the generators a,b and hence on all of P.\nThus the maps are inverse isomorphisms. No extra relation between the\nintegers remains.</p>\n<p>Combining N1–N4 proves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2</annotation></semantics></math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>▫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "example": "<p>In the product model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=S^1\\times S^1</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith basepoint (1,1), define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_{m,n}(t)=(e^{2\\pi imt},e^{2\\pi int}),\\qquad0\\le t\\le1.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Its class corresponds to (m,n). The coordinate projection maps send a\nto winding number 1 in the first circle and b to winding number 1 in the\nsecond, so they realize the same Phi constructed in N4.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Loop word</th>\n<th>Integer pair</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>(1,0)</td>\n<td>Horizontal generator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b</td>\n<td>(0,1)</td>\n<td>Vertical generator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^{2}b^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Not null-homotopic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">aba^{(-1)}b^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>(0,0)</td>\n<td>Killed by the attached face</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^{2}b^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nfollowed by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^{(-1)}b^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>(1,2)</td>\n<td>Concatenation becomes addition</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Because Phi is an isomorphism, a loop is null-homotopic exactly when\nboth integer coordinates vanish. The zero-pair conclusion is supported\nby injectivity, not merely by observing two zero winding numbers.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>A cover with disconnected intersection does not satisfy the stated\nversion of van Kampen. Reading the face word as ab instead of the\ncommutator changes the presentation and yields a different calculation.\nHomology abelianization alone cannot recover an arbitrary fundamental\ngroup; here the presentation proves that the full group itself is\nabelian.</p>\n<p>Export the square cell model, free one-skeleton group, explicit\nattaching-relation calculation, and isomorphism with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe same cell structure supports the torus homology problem, where\nsigned incidences give a different but compatible invariant.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>A failed branch abelianizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nbefore accounting for the face, claiming that any two-loop graph has\ngroup\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> N2</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>State:</strong> STALLED</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Failing component:</strong> Execution</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Failed claim:</strong> wedge group is Z squared</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Observed:</strong> commutator is nontrivial reduced word\nbefore face attachment</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Repair target:</strong> cell-attachment\ndependency</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Blocked export:</strong> torus group without filling\nargument</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Knowledge retrieves the free-product conclusion of van Kampen. Validation\ndistinguishes the one-skeleton from the whole torus. Strategy restores\nthe face-attachment branch. Execution applies the valid open cover, imposes\nthe normal commutator relation, and checks both inverse maps. Contract\npublishes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nonly after this dependency is satisfied.</p>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>A cover with disconnected intersection does not satisfy the stated\nversion of van Kampen. Reading the face word as ab instead of the\ncommutator changes the presentation and yields a different calculation.\nHomology abelianization alone cannot recover an arbitrary fundamental\ngroup; here the presentation proves that the full group itself is\nabelian.</p>\n<p>Export the square cell model, free one-skeleton group, explicit\nattaching-relation calculation, and isomorphism with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe same cell structure supports the torus homology problem, where\nsigned incidences give a different but compatible invariant.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Version changed contracts and recheck the revised export and every\ndependent node. Diagnostic feedback is not a proof premise.</p>"}], "standard": false, "sha256": "c2c64fc0e3f995c13a46d157058a74f5d1634f1ffc3e2623075a90c8c746d89c", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of fundamental group of the torus via\nseifert–van kampen, with explicit mathematical contracts, local\nverification gates, and reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATchapters.html\">Allen\nHatcher, Algebraic Topology, Chapter 1: van Kampen and cell\nattachments</a>.</p>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/fundamental-group-of-torus.pdf"}, {"id": "AT-03", "slug": "homology-of-the-torus", "title": "Homology of the Torus", "target": "<p>Compute integral homology of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2</annotation></semantics></math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0\\cong\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1\\cong\\mathbb Z^2</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_2\\cong\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_k=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use the square quotient CW structure on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:\none vertexv, oriented edgesa,b, and one2-cellc attached alongaba\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi></mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nImport cellular homology with integral coefficients:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_k</annotation></semantics></math>\nis free on k-cells, and its differential is the attaching-map incidence\ndegree after collapsing the other cells. Cellular homology agrees with\nsingular homology. AT-02 supplies this model; its\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nresult is not required for the boundary computation.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Chain groups and edge boundary", "proof": "1. The cellular chain groups areC₂=Z⟨c⟩, C₁=Z⟨a,b⟩, C₀=Z⟨v⟩ and zero in higher degrees.\n\n2. Both endpoints of each edge arev, with opposite endpoint signs, so∂₁a=v−v=0 and∂₁b=0.", "role": "<p>Count cells and compute endpoint incidences.</p>", "inputs": "<p>The CW cell model; foundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>The chain groups and zero degree 1 differential.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E1/v1: The chain groups and zero degree 1 differential.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain The CW cell model; foundational imports stated in §1; keep\ntheir assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Count cells and compute endpoint incidences.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Identified endpoints still retain their opposite incidence signs.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>The cellular chain groups are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{2}=Z\\langle c\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{1}=Z\\langle a,b\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{0}=Z\\langle v\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>\nand zero in higher degrees.</p>", "<p>Both endpoints of each edge arev, with opposite endpoint signs, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{1}a=v-v=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{1}b=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Identified endpoints still retain their opposite incidence signs.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Identified endpoints still retain\ntheir opposite incidence signs. Recheck dependent nodes N2, N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Face boundary", "proof": "1. Collapse all1-cells excepta when computing the coefficient ofa in∂₂c.\n\n2. The attaching loop crossesa once forward and once backward, giving degree1−1=0.\n\n3. The same calculation for b gives 0.\n\n4. Therefore∂₂c=0a+0b.\n\n5. This is a signed incidence computation, not an assumption that every closed surface has zero cellular boundary in every chosen decomposition.", "role": "<p>Compute signed degree separately for each target edge.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Attaching wordaba\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi></mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>The degree 2 differential is zero.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E2/v1: The degree 2 differential is zero.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Attaching wordaba\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi></mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Compute signed degree separately for each target edge.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Counting appearances without orientation would incorrectly\nproduce2a+2b.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Collapse all 1-cells excepta when computing the coefficient ofa in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{2}c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The attaching loop crossesa once forward and once backward, giving\ndegree\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The same calculation for b gives 0.</p>", "<p>Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{2}c=0a+0b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>This is a signed incidence computation, not an assumption that every\nclosed surface has zero cellular boundary in every chosen\ndecomposition.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Counting appearances without orientation would incorrectly\nproduce2a+2b. Publish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Counting appearances without\norientation would incorrectly produce2a+2b. Recheck dependent nodes\nN3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Homology and independent checks", "proof": "1. The chain complex is 0→Z→⁰Z²→⁰Z→0, soH₂=ker∂₂=Z, H₁=ker∂₁/im∂₂=Z², H₀=C₀/im∂₁=Z, andH_k=0 for k>2.\n\n2. Its alternating rank sum1−2+1=0 matches the cell count.\n\n3. If independently using AT-02, H₁ matches the abelianization of π₁(T²)=Z².\n\n4. These checks support consistency but the kernel/image computation establishes the groups.", "role": "<p>Compute kernels modulo images in each degree.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Both zero differentials; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>All integral homology groups in the target.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E3/v1: All integral homology groups in the target.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Both zero differentials; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1; keep\ntheir assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Compute kernels modulo images in each degree.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Euler characteristic alone cannot determine every homology group or\nits torsion.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>The chain complex is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></msup><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\to Z\\to ^{0}Z^{2}\\to ^{0}Z\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}=\\operatorname{ker} \\partial _{2}=Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">im</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{1}=\\operatorname{ker} \\partial _{1}/\\operatorname{im} \\partial _{2}=Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">im</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{0}=C_{0}/\\operatorname{im} \\partial _{1}=Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_k=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Its alternating rank sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-2+1=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nmatches the cell count.</p>", "<p>If independently using AT-02,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nmatches the abelianization of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}(T^{2})=Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>These checks support consistency but the kernel/image computation\nestablishes the groups.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Euler characteristic alone cannot determine every homology group or\nits torsion. Publish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Euler characteristic alone cannot\ndetermine every homology group or its torsion. Withhold this terminal\nresult until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Face boundary", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Attaching wordaba\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi></mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}b^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Homology and independent checks", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Both zero differentials; prior exports\nE1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N3: Homology and independent checks", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Both zero differentials; prior exports\nE1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-chain-groups-and-edge-boundary-1\">§4.N1 — Chain groups and\nedge boundary</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>The cellular chain groups are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{2}=Z\\langle c\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{1}=Z\\langle a,b\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{0}=Z\\langle v\\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>\nand zero in higher degrees.</p></li>\n<li><p>Both endpoints of each edge arev, with opposite endpoint signs,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{1}a=v-v=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{1}b=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-face-boundary-1\">§4.N2 — Face boundary</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Collapse all 1-cells excepta when computing the coefficient ofa\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{2}c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The attaching loop crossesa once forward and once backward,\ngiving degree\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The same calculation for b gives 0.</p></li>\n<li><p>Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{2}c=0a+0b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>This is a signed incidence computation, not an assumption that\nevery closed surface has zero cellular boundary in every chosen\ndecomposition.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-homology-and-independent-checks-1\">§4.N3 — Homology and\nindependent checks</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>The chain complex is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></msup><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\to Z\\to ^{0}Z^{2}\\to ^{0}Z\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}=\\operatorname{ker} \\partial _{2}=Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">im</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{1}=\\operatorname{ker} \\partial _{1}/\\operatorname{im} \\partial _{2}=Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">im</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{0}=C_{0}/\\operatorname{im} \\partial _{1}=Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_k=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Its alternating rank sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-2+1=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nmatches the cell count.</p></li>\n<li><p>If independently using AT-02,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nmatches the abelianization of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{1}(T^{2})=Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>These checks support consistency but the kernel/image computation\nestablishes the groups.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>The oriented2-cellc is a generator of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nbecause its boundary is 0 and there are no3-cells to make it a boundary.\nThe classesa,b independently generateH\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi></mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nan integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">cycle3a-2b</annotation></semantics></math>\nis nonzero because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">im</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{im} \\partial _{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RP^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nthe 2-cell attaches by a degree 2 loop, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis multiplication by 2 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{1}=Z/2</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.\nSense uses this comparison to reject the claim that all face boundaries\nvanish merely because an attaching word is closed.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Contract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge + Strategy:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Contract:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RP^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nthe 2-cell attaches by a degree 2 loop, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial _{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis multiplication by 2 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{1}=Z/2</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.\nSense uses this comparison to reject the claim that all face boundaries\nvanish merely because an attaching word is closed.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "ace3d6c354bfdf5a359fc5824a8d5efd8f5e9038ff1cf2061ebd2ba6e39c281e", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of homology of the torus, with\nexplicit mathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf\">Allen\nHatcher, Algebraic Topology: covering spaces, cellular homology, and cap\nproducts</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/homology-of-the-torus.pdf"}, {"id": "AT-04", "slug": "brouwer-fixed-point-theorem-via-no-retraction", "title": "Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem via No Retraction", "target": "<p>For every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:D^n\\to D^n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a fixed point by deriving a forbidden retraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^n\\to S^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nfrom a fixed-point-free map.</p>", "imports": ["<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the closed Euclidean unit ball and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nits boundary,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge 1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nImport homotopy invariance of reduced singular homology, functoriality,\ncontractibility of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand the sphere computation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>H</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde H_{n-1}(S^{(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=Z</annotation></semantics></math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nthis is reduced\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{0}(S^{0})=Z</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwhile reduced\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{0}(D^{1})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe no-retraction result is proved below.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "A fixed-point-free map produces a retraction", "proof": "1. Putv=x−f(x)≠0.\n\n2. The positive endpoint of the rayy=f(x)+tv on the unit sphere is obtained at t(x)=[−f(x)·v+√((f(x)·v)²+(1−‖f(x)‖²)‖v‖²)]/‖v‖².\n\n3. The denominator is nonzero.\n\n4. This is the larger root of‖f(x)+tv‖²=1, and t≥1 because x at t=1 lies in the ball.\n\n5. If f(x) lies on the sphere thenf(x)·v<0 unlessx=f(x), so the chosen root is still positive.\n\n6. The displayed expression is continuous, andr(x)=f(x)+t(x)v is sphere-valued.\n\n7. Ifx is on the sphere, t=1: the ball’s ray intersection ends there, since distinct points of a Euclidean sphere have a strictly interior connecting segment.\n\n8. Thusr(x)=x on the boundary, giving a retraction.", "role": "<p>Extend the ray from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>\nthroughx to its last sphere intersection.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Continuousf:D^{n}\\to D^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\ne x</annotation></semantics></math>\nassumed for contradiction; foundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>A continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">retractionr:D^{n}\\to S^{(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nunder the no-fixed-point assumption.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E1/v1: A continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">retractionr:D^{n}\\to S^{(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nunder the no-fixed-point assumption.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Continuousf:D^{n}\\to D^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\ne x</annotation></semantics></math>\nassumed for contradiction; foundational imports stated in §1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Extend the ray from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>\nthroughx to its last sphere intersection.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Continuity and the boundary identity must both be proved.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Put\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=x-f(x)\\ne 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The positive endpoint of the ray\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(x)+tv</annotation></semantics></math>\non the unit sphere is obtained at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>−</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x)=[-f(x)\\cdot v+\\sqrt{((f(x)\\cdot v)^{2}}+(1-\\Vert f(x)\\Vert ^{2})\\Vert v\\Vert ^{2})]/\\Vert v\\Vert ^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The denominator is nonzero.</p>", "<p>This is the larger root of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert f(x)+tv\\Vert ^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge 1</annotation></semantics></math>\nbecause x at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in the ball.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies on the sphere then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\cdot v&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nunless\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the chosen root is still positive.</p>", "<p>The displayed expression is continuous, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(x)=f(x)+t(x)v</annotation></semantics></math>\nis sphere-valued.</p>", "<p>Ifx is on the sphere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>:\nthe ball’s ray intersection ends there, since distinct points of a\nEuclidean sphere have a strictly interior connecting segment.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(x)=x</annotation></semantics></math>\non the boundary, giving a retraction.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Continuity and the boundary identity must both be proved. Publish\nonly after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Continuity and the boundary identity\nmust both be proved. Recheck dependent nodes N2.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "No retraction by reduced homology", "proof": "1. A retraction would satisfyr∘i=id_S.\n\n2. Hence r_*∘i_*=id onHtilde_(n−1)(S;Z)=Z.\n\n3. Buti_* lands inHtilde_(n−1)(D;Z)=0, so the composite is the zero map.\n\n4. It cannot equal the identity onZ.\n\n5. This contradiction excludes the retraction, thus excludes a fixed-point-freef.\n\n6. Every continuous disk self-map has a fixed point, including n=1.", "role": "<p>Apply functoriality in the one degree that separates disk and\nsphere.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Retractionr and boundary inclusioni; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Brouwer’s fixed-point theorem in every stated dimension.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E2/v1: Brouwer’s fixed-point theorem in every stated\ndimension.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Retractionr and boundary inclusioni; prior exports E1/v1; keep\ntheir assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Apply functoriality in the one degree that separates disk and\nsphere.</p>", "Validation": "<p>OrdinaryH\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi></mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>\nof a disk is Z; reduced homology is essential for the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nversion of this argument.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>A retraction would satisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">id</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\circ i=\\operatorname{id} _S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_*\\circ i_*=\\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>H</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde H_{n-1}(S</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>But\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_*</annotation></semantics></math>\nlands in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>H</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde H_{n-1}(D</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the composite is the zero map.</p>", "<p>It cannot equal the identity on Z.</p>", "<p>This contradiction excludes the retraction, thus excludes a\nfixed-point-freef.</p>", "<p>Every continuous disk self-map has a fixed point, including\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>OrdinaryH\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi></mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>\nof a disk is Z; reduced homology is essential for the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nversion of this argument. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: OrdinaryH\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi></mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>\nof a disk is Z; reduced homology is essential for the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nversion of this argument. Withhold this terminal result until its gate\npasses.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: No retraction by reduced homology", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Retractionr and boundary inclusioni; prior\nexports E1/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-a-fixed-point-free-map-produces-a-retraction-1\">§4.N1 — A\nfixed-point-free map produces a retraction</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Put\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=x-f(x)\\ne 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The positive endpoint of the ray\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(x)+tv</annotation></semantics></math>\non the unit sphere is obtained at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>−</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x)=[-f(x)\\cdot v+\\sqrt{((f(x)\\cdot v)^{2}}+(1-\\Vert f(x)\\Vert ^{2})\\Vert v\\Vert ^{2})]/\\Vert v\\Vert ^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The denominator is nonzero.</p></li>\n<li><p>This is the larger root of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert f(x)+tv\\Vert ^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge 1</annotation></semantics></math>\nbecause x at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in the ball.</p></li>\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies on the sphere then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\cdot v&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nunless\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the chosen root is still positive.</p></li>\n<li><p>The displayed expression is continuous, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(x)=f(x)+t(x)v</annotation></semantics></math>\nis sphere-valued.</p></li>\n<li><p>Ifx is on the sphere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>:\nthe ball’s ray intersection ends there, since distinct points of a\nEuclidean sphere have a strictly interior connecting segment.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(x)=x</annotation></semantics></math>\non the boundary, giving a retraction.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-no-retraction-by-reduced-homology-1\">§4.N2 — No retraction by\nreduced homology</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>A retraction would satisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">id</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\circ i=\\operatorname{id} _S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_*\\circ i_*=\\operatorname{id}</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>H</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde H_{n-1}(S</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>But\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_*</annotation></semantics></math>\nlands in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>H</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde H_{n-1}(D</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the composite is the zero map.</p></li>\n<li><p>It cannot equal the identity on Z.</p></li>\n<li><p>This contradiction excludes the retraction, thus excludes a\nfixed-point-freef.</p></li>\n<li><p>Every continuous disk self-map has a fixed point, including\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>The constant map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert a\\Vert \\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>\nfixes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=a</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe affine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">contractionf(x)=x/2</annotation></semantics></math>\nalso fixes 0. In either example the hypothetical retraction formula\nfails at its fixed point because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nexactly the point the contradiction proves must exist.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>A rotation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nby a nonzero angle has no fixed point, so a sphere self-map does not\nsatisfy the disk theorem. The corresponding rotation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\ndoes fix 0. Validation retains both the disk domain and self-map condition\nrather than extending the claim to arbitrary compact spaces.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Contract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge + Strategy:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Contract:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>A rotation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nby a nonzero angle has no fixed point, so a sphere self-map does not\nsatisfy the disk theorem. The corresponding rotation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\ndoes fix 0. Validation retains both the disk domain and self-map condition\nrather than extending the claim to arbitrary compact spaces.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "6ba5979e1f26df2f6138868c62ea12c63e16a3c5725025f2f4e1e3dfb04ae45e", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of brouwer fixed-point theorem via no\nretraction, with explicit mathematical contracts, local verification\ngates, and reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf\">Allen\nHatcher, Algebraic Topology: covering spaces, cellular homology, and cap\nproducts</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/brouwer-fixed-point-theorem-via-no-retraction.pdf"}, {"id": "AT-05", "slug": "poincare-duality", "title": "Poincaré Duality", "target": "<p>For a closed connected oriented smooth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove cap product with its integral fundamental class gives an\nisomorphism\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^k(M;\\mathbb Z)\\to H_{n-k}(M;\\mathbb Z)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le k\\le n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use a finite smooth triangulationK of the closed oriented manifold,\ncompatible orientations on top simplices, and its barycentric\nsubdivision. Import simplicial/cellular-to-singular homology and\ncohomology comparison and subdivision invariance. In a triangulated\nmanifold the closed star of each simplex is contractible, and\nbarycentric dual blocks form a cell decomposition: the link is a sphere\nand coning its subdivided chains gives the complementary-dimensional\nball. Use the chain definition of cap product with identity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial ([M]</annotation></semantics></math>\ncap\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha )=(-1)^{(k+1)}[M]</annotation></semantics></math>\ncap\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta \\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor a closed fundamental cycle and a degree-k cochain, with a fixed\nmatching cap convention. No duality is imported; the dual differential\nand identification are established below.</p>\n<p>Use a compatible smooth (hence combinatorial) triangulation, so links\nhave the required sphere structure. The singular-chain filling in a\nclosed star is provided by a contraction of that star, not by a claim\nthat an arbitrary contractible space is literally a geometric cone.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Fundamental cycle and dual blocks", "proof": "1. Letc be the sum of compatibly orientedn-simplices.\n\n2. Every(n−1)-face is shared twice with opposite induced signs, so∂c=0.\n\n3. Its local coefficient at every oriented manifold point is+1, so it represents the integral fundamental class[M].\n\n4. For an orientedk-simplexσ, its dual blockDσ is the union of barycentric flags beginning at σ and continuing through cofaces up to top-dimensional simplices.\n\n5. It has dimensionn−k.\n\n6. OrientDσ so the ordered orientation of σ followed by its transverse dual orientation gives the orientation ofM.\n\n7. These blocks bijectively pair k-simplices with(n−k)-cells.", "role": "<p>Pair an oriented simplex with its transverse dual block.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Oriented triangulation of a closedn-manifold; foundational imports\nstated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>The fundamental cycle and a basis pairing between simplicial cochains\nand dual cellular chains.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E1/v1: The fundamental cycle and a basis pairing between\nsimplicial cochains and dual cellular chains.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Oriented triangulation of a closedn-manifold; foundational\nimports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Pair an oriented simplex with its transverse dual block.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Closedness cancels all faces; orientability makes their signs\nglobally compatible.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Letc be the sum of compatibly orientedn-simplices.</p>", "<p>Every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-face\nis shared twice with opposite induced signs, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial c=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Its local coefficient at every oriented manifold point is+1, so it\nrepresents the integral fundamental class[M].</p>", "<p>For an orientedk-simplex\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,\nits dual blockD\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the union of barycentric flags beginning at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nand continuing through cofaces up to top-dimensional simplices.</p>", "<p>It has dimension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>OrientD\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nso the ordered orientation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nfollowed by its transverse dual orientation gives the orientation of\nM.</p>", "<p>These blocks bijectively pair k-simplices with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>-cells.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Closedness cancels all faces; orientability makes their signs\nglobally compatible. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Closedness cancels all faces;\norientability makes their signs globally compatible. Recheck dependent\nnodes N2, N3, N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Compute the dual-chain differential", "proof": "1. The codimension-one boundary faces ofDσ correspond to(k+1)-cofacesτ of σ and are copies ofDτ.\n\n2. Other flag faces occur twice with opposite subdivision signs and cancel.\n\n3. If[τ:σ] is the signed simplex incidence, moving the normal-to-σ direction across itsk tangent directions and using outward-first boundary orientation gives the coefficient(−1)^(k+1)[τ:σ].\n\n4. Thus ∂Dσ=(−1)^(k+1)∑_{τ⊃σ}[τ:σ]Dτ.\n\n5. For the cochain basisσ*, δσ*=∑[τ:σ]τ*.\n\n6. Hence the degree-reversing basis mapD satisfies ∂Dα=(−1)^(k+1)Dδα.\n\n7. It sends cocycles to cycles and coboundaries to boundaries, with an inverse on bases and the same differential relation.", "role": "<p>Compare boundary flags for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nand its immediate cofaces.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Oriented dual blocks and incidence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">numbers[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>A degree-reversing chain identification inducing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^k(K</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)\\cong H_{n-k}(dualK</annotation></semantics></math>;Z).</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E2/v1: A degree-reversing chain identification inducing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^k(K</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)\\cong H_{n-k}(dualK</annotation></semantics></math>;Z).</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Oriented dual blocks and incidence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">numbers[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Compare boundary flags for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nand its immediate cofaces.</p>", "Validation": "<p>Ignoring signs would only justify a mod 2 statement, not integral\nduality.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>The codimension-one boundary faces of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\ncorrespond to (k+1)-cofaces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nand are copies of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Other flag faces occur twice with opposite subdivision signs and\ncancel.</p>", "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">If[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the signed simplex incidence, moving the\nnormal-to-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\ndirection across itsk tangent directions and using outward-first\nboundary orientation gives the coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1)^{(k+1)}[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial D\\sigma =(-1)^{(k+1)}\\sum _{\\tau \\supset \\sigma }[\\tau :\\sigma ]D\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>For the cochain basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>*</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma *</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mi>*</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>*</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta \\sigma *=\\sum [\\tau :\\sigma ]\\tau *</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Hence the degree-reversing basis map D satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>D</mi><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial D\\alpha =(-1)^{(k+1)}D\\delta \\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>It sends cocycles to cycles and coboundaries to boundaries, with an\ninverse on bases and the same differential relation.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Ignoring signs would only justify a mod 2 statement, not integral\nduality. Publish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Ignoring signs would only justify a\nmod 2 statement, not integral duality. Recheck dependent nodes N3,\nN4.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Identify the induced map with cap product", "proof": "1. View the reversed cochain complex as a chain complex with degree r=n−k and differential(−1)^(k+1)δ.\n\n2. After mapping dual cellular chains to singular chains in the subdivision, bothD andC:α↦c capα are chain maps to singular chains.\n\n3. For a basisσ* both images are supported in the closed star of σ: dual flags lie there, and every cap term comes from a top simplex containingσ.\n\n4. The carrier is nested under the differential since cofaces have stars contained in that of σ.\n\n5. In degree0, for a top-simplex cochain, the dual barycenter and cap-selected vertex have the same oriented augmentation.\n\n6. Construct a chain homotopy upward on this free basis: if H is already defined one degree lower, z=(D−C)(σ*)−H(dσ*) is a cycle in starσ, with zero augmentation when it is a0-cycle.\n\n7. The star is contractible, so this cycle bounds; use the induced singular-chain contraction to choose a filling $H(\\sigma^*)$.\n\n8. Inductively D−C=∂H+Hd.\n\n9. Thus the two maps induce the same map on homology.\n\n10. This is the explicit local-carrier comparison between dual blocks and the cap construction.", "role": "<p>Use local carriers to compare two chain constructions, not only\nabstract ranks.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Dual map D and cap map againstc; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>The dual-cell isomorphism is the cap-product map [M]\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">cap(-)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Identify cap product with the dual-cell isomorphism.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Import E1/v1, E2/v1, the cap-chain formula, and contractible closed\nstars.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Use local carriers to compare two chain constructions, not only\nabstract ranks.</p>", "Validation": "<p>An abstract group isomorphism would not identify the specified\ncanonical map.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Build the carrier homotopy and publish E3/v1.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>View the reversed cochain complex as a chain complex with degree\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=n-k</annotation></semantics></math>\nand differential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1)^{(k+1)}\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>After mapping dual cellular chains to singular chains in the\nsubdivision, both D and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C:\\alpha \\mapsto c</annotation></semantics></math>\ncap\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nare chain maps to singular chains.</p>", "<p>For a basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>*</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma *</annotation></semantics></math>\nboth images are supported in the closed star of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>:\ndual flags lie there, and every cap term comes from a top simplex\ncontaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The carrier is nested under the differential since cofaces have stars\ncontained in that of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>In degree 0, for a top-simplex cochain, the dual barycenter and\ncap-selected vertex have the same oriented augmentation.</p>", "<p>Construct a chain homotopy upward on this free basis: if H is already\ndefined one degree lower,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>*</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>σ</mi><mi>*</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(D-C)(\\sigma *)-H(d\\sigma *)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a cycle in star\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith zero augmentation when it is a0-cycle.</p>", "<p>The star is contractible, so this cycle bounds; use the induced\nsingular-chain contraction to choose a filling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(\\sigma^*)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Inductively\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∂</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D-C=\\partial H+Hd</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus the two maps induce the same map on homology.</p>", "<p>This is the explicit local-carrier comparison between dual blocks and\nthe cap construction.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Identify the canonical cap-product map, not merely isomorphic\ngroups.</p>", "repair": "<p>Rebuild the carrier homotopy, then recheck N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Transfer to singular groups and close", "proof": "1. Simplicial cohomology ofK and cellular homology of its dual decomposition identify with the respective singular groups ofM.\n\n2. These comparison maps commute with subdivision and cap products by the chain constructions used in node3.\n\n3. Since D is an isomorphism on the reversed complexes, it is an isomorphism on their homology.\n\n4. Since C induces the same map, cap product with[M] gives H^k(M;Z)→H_(n−k)(M;Z) as an isomorphism for every0≤k≤n.\n\n5. Reversing orientation negates[M] and the resulting map but preserves its invertibility.", "role": "<p>Apply the imported comparison maps compatibly.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Chain identification and cap comparison; prior exports E2/v1,\nE3/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Integral Poincaré duality with the specified fundamental-class cap\nmap.</p>", "components": {"Contract": "<p>Own E4/v1: Integral Poincaré duality with the specified\nfundamental-class cap map.</p>", "Knowledge": "<p>Retain Chain identification and cap comparison; prior exports E2/v1,\nE3/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Strategy": "<p>Apply the imported comparison maps compatibly.</p>", "Validation": "<p>The theorem is for closed oriented manifolds; boundary and\nnonorientability change the target coefficients or relative groups.</p>", "Execution": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E4/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Simplicial cohomology of K and cellular homology of its dual\ndecomposition identify with the respective singular groups of M.</p>", "<p>These comparison maps commute with subdivision and cap products by\nthe chain constructions used in node3.</p>", "<p>Since D is an isomorphism on the reversed complexes, it is an\nisomorphism on their homology.</p>", "<p>Since C induces the same map, cap product with[M] gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^k(M</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)\\to H_{n-k}(M</annotation></semantics></math>;Z)\nas an isomorphism for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le k\\le n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Reversing orientation negates[M] and the resulting map but preserves\nits invertibility.</p>"], "exportId": "E4", "gate": "<p>The theorem is for closed oriented manifolds; boundary and\nnonorientability change the target coefficients or relative groups.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N4: The theorem is for closed oriented\nmanifolds; boundary and nonorientability change the target coefficients\nor relative groups. Withhold this terminal result until its gate\npasses.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Compute the dual-chain differential", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Oriented dual blocks and incidence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">numbers[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Identify the induced map with cap product", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Dual map D and cap map againstc; prior\nexports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N3: Identify the induced map with cap product", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Dual map D and cap map againstc; prior\nexports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N4", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T4", "activity": "N4: Transfer to singular groups and close", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Chain identification and cap comparison;\nprior exports E2/v1, E3/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N3", "target": "N4", "export": "E3/v1", "id": "T5", "activity": "N4: Transfer to singular groups and close", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Chain identification and cap comparison;\nprior exports E2/v1, E3/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-fundamental-cycle-and-dual-blocks-1\">§4.N1 — Fundamental\ncycle and dual blocks</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Letc be the sum of compatibly orientedn-simplices.</p></li>\n<li><p>Every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-face\nis shared twice with opposite induced signs, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial c=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Its local coefficient at every oriented manifold point is+1, so\nit represents the integral fundamental class[M].</p></li>\n<li><p>For an orientedk-simplex\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,\nits dual blockD\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the union of barycentric flags beginning at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nand continuing through cofaces up to top-dimensional simplices.</p></li>\n<li><p>It has dimension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>OrientD\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nso the ordered orientation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nfollowed by its transverse dual orientation gives the orientation of\nM.</p></li>\n<li><p>These blocks bijectively pair k-simplices with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>-cells.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-compute-the-dual-chain-differential-1\">§4.N2 — Compute the\ndual-chain differential</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>The codimension-one boundary faces of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\ncorrespond to (k+1)-cofaces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\nand are copies of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Other flag faces occur twice with opposite subdivision signs and\ncancel.</p></li>\n<li><p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">If[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the signed simplex incidence, moving the\nnormal-to-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>\ndirection across itsk tangent directions and using outward-first\nboundary orientation gives the coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1)^{(k+1)}[\\tau :\\sigma ]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial D\\sigma =(-1)^{(k+1)}\\sum _{\\tau \\supset \\sigma }[\\tau :\\sigma ]D\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>For the cochain basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>*</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma *</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mi>*</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>*</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta \\sigma *=\\sum [\\tau :\\sigma ]\\tau *</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hence the degree-reversing basis map D satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>D</mi><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial D\\alpha =(-1)^{(k+1)}D\\delta \\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>It sends cocycles to cycles and coboundaries to boundaries, with\nan inverse on bases and the same differential relation.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-identify-the-induced-map-with-cap-product-1\">§4.N3 — Identify\nthe induced map with cap product</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>View the reversed cochain complex as a chain complex with degree\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=n-k</annotation></semantics></math>\nand differential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1)^{(k+1)}\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>After mapping dual cellular chains to singular chains in the\nsubdivision, both D and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C:\\alpha \\mapsto c</annotation></semantics></math>\ncap\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>\nare chain maps to singular chains.</p></li>\n<li><p>For a basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>*</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma *</annotation></semantics></math>\nboth images are supported in the closed star of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>:\ndual flags lie there, and every cap term comes from a top simplex\ncontaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The carrier is nested under the differential since cofaces have\nstars contained in that of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>In degree 0, for a top-simplex cochain, the dual barycenter and\ncap-selected vertex have the same oriented augmentation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Construct a chain homotopy upward on this free basis: if H is\nalready defined one degree lower,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>*</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>σ</mi><mi>*</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(D-C)(\\sigma *)-H(d\\sigma *)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a cycle in star\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith zero augmentation when it is a0-cycle.</p></li>\n<li><p>The star is contractible, so this cycle bounds; use the induced\nsingular-chain contraction to choose a filling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(\\sigma^*)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Inductively\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∂</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D-C=\\partial H+Hd</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus the two maps induce the same map on homology.</p></li>\n<li><p>This is the explicit local-carrier comparison between dual blocks\nand the cap construction.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n4-transfer-to-singular-groups-and-close-1\">§4.N4 — Transfer to\nsingular groups and close</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Simplicial cohomology of K and cellular homology of its dual\ndecomposition identify with the respective singular groups of\nM.</p></li>\n<li><p>These comparison maps commute with subdivision and cap products\nby the chain constructions used in node3.</p></li>\n<li><p>Since D is an isomorphism on the reversed complexes, it is an\nisomorphism on their homology.</p></li>\n<li><p>Since C induces the same map, cap product with[M] gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^k(M</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)\\to H_{n-k}(M</annotation></semantics></math>;Z)\nas an isomorphism for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le k\\le n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Reversing orientation negates[M] and the resulting map but\npreserves its invertibility.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>On an oriented torus,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^{0}=Z</annotation></semantics></math>\npairs with the fundamental2-cycle,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^{2}=Z</annotation></semantics></math>\npairs with a point class, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^{1}=Z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\npairs with the two complementary1-cycle directions. Using product\norientation, the degree 1 classes are exchanged with complementary\ncycles up to the cap convention’s signs. The dual-block proof supplies\nthe isomorphism rather than only comparing these ranks.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>For a disk,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^{0}(D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=Z</annotation></semantics></math>\nbut\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}(D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the absolute closed-manifold formula fails. For a nonorientable\nclosed manifold an untwisted integral fundamental class need not exist.\nSense blocks the initial fundamental-cycle gate; relative or\nlocal-coefficient duality is a distinct theorem.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Contract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge + Strategy:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Contract:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>For a disk,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^{0}(D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=Z</annotation></semantics></math>\nbut\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{2}(D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the absolute closed-manifold formula fails. For a nonorientable\nclosed manifold an untwisted integral fundamental class need not exist.\nSense blocks the initial fundamental-cycle gate; relative or\nlocal-coefficient duality is a distinct theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "ebba382fa4f53079cfd3c04ad38af81b76fc6fbef24b8859a1afe865b7ac55b2", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of poincaré duality, with explicit\nmathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf\">Allen\nHatcher, Algebraic Topology: covering spaces, cellular homology, and cap\nproducts</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/poincare-duality.pdf"}], "links": [{"source": "AT-01", "target": "AT-02", "label": "Circle fundamental group", "detail": "Van Kampen uses the circle group for the wedge and the punctured disk."}, {"source": "AT-02", "target": "AT-03", "label": "CW structure export", "detail": "The square model and its attaching word determine cellular boundary maps; the full π₁ theorem is not needed."}], "evidence": "Authored proof walkthroughs. No deployed agent execution or formal kernel verification is claimed."};
